# UI changes

A PR that changes anything a user sees on screen — chrome, layout, copy,
colour tokens that surface in CSS, focus order, header / footer / drawer
behaviour — is a **UI PR** even if its `git diff` looks small. A
one-line token rename that re-weights a dozen surfaces is a UI PR. A
hover-state tweak is a UI PR. The shape of the diff does not determine
the category; the visible change does.

## What a UI PR must include

### 1. `pnpm verify:ui-evidence` output (or its updated counterpart)

For PRs that touch the mobile nav drawer or any chrome component
covered by `scripts/ui-evidence.mjs`, the existing harness must run
clean against the diff's tip — paste the harness's console output or
`results.json` summary in the PR body. A regression caught pre-merge is
cheap; the same regression caught in production requires a hotfix PR,
a re-bookkeeping cycle, and a fresh UI-evidence baseline run.

For PRs that introduce new chrome without an existing harness coverage,
the PR author must either (a) extend `ui-evidence.mjs` in the same PR
to cover the new surface, or (b) explain in the PR body what visual
checks they did perform. **Green CI does not substitute** — the UI
sharness is its own gate for chrome-touching changes.

### 2. Screenshots in the PR body

At least three:

- Desktop ≥1280 wide
- A mobile viewport that exercises the change (375 by default; 768 if
  the diff targets tablet specifically)
- The "before" state — either as a separate screenshot or as an inline
  contrast in the write-up

A screenshot is the only way a reviewer who did not run the diff can
verify that the change does what the description claims. Annotations
(diffs, arrows, callouts) help but are not required. **Screenshots from
PR preview** are fine; screenshots from a fork or local screenshot farm
are not — they lie about the actual deployed branch's chrome.

### 3. Self-check before opening the PR

- `pnpm typecheck`
- `pnpm lint` (if the diff touches TSX / CSS)
- `pnpm verify:ui-evidence`
- Functional probe of any interactive surface the PR adds or modifies
  (drawer open / close, theme toggle round-trip, focus trap). The CDP
  recipe and chrome probes are in `scripts/ui-evidence.mjs` and the
  `corpus-web-context` skill's `references/dialog-gotchas.md`.

### 4. PR body "What does this PR claim, and what checks that claim against reality?"

This is the **PR template's first question** (see
`.github/pull_request_template.md`). It is non-negotiable for UI PRs.

- One sentence per claim.
- For each claim, one concrete check that verifies it (screenshot,
  harness line, manual probe). The check must be reproducible by
  someone who has not seen the diff.
- "Eyeballed it" or "looks good in Vercel preview" is not a check. It
  is a feeling; the gate exists to convert feelings into receipts.

## Why this exists

The chrome polish work in Session 215 surfaced real bugs (drawer
backdrop creating a containing block for `position: fixed`,
`aria-controls` propagation missing on the trigger, focus-trap race
that escaped to skip-to-content on Tab past the second-to-last
focusable). All three were caught before merge by the
ui-evidence harness that the chrome polish also introduced.

Without the rule, the harness stays a habit; a habit lasts as long as
the agent that built it. With the rule + the PR template question,
the harness is the **definition of done** for chrome work.
